#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079547} :activity/id #uuid "92a67e2b-bdab-5391-a359-7c186fb75ce2" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048202 :topic/id #uuid "87acae7d-39e1-5216-acf9-966560dccc34" :topic/math-academy-id 650} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079547 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079672 :tutorial/content "A differential operator $D$ is an operator that acts on a function $y(x)$ by forming an expression involving $y$ and one or more of its derivatives.\n\nFor example, consider the following differential operator.\n\n$$\nD(y)=\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{2}y}{\\textrm d x^{2}}+2\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm d x} + y\n$$\n\nThis is a **second-order differential operator** because the highest derivative is of order $2.$ \n\nWe can apply $D$ to any function $y(x)$ that's twice-differentiable. For example, let's apply the operator to $y(x) = \\sin x.$ We substitute this function into the operator and calculate the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(\\operatorname{sin}x) & =\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(\\operatorname{sin}x)+2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}x)+\\operatorname{sin}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}x)+2\\operatorname{cos}x+\\operatorname{sin}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}x+2\\operatorname{cos}x+\\operatorname{sin}x \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can think of our operator $D$ as a transformation that takes a *function* $y(x)$ as input and produces *another function* as output. In this example, the operator $D$ took the function $\\sin x$ and mapped it to the function $2\\cos x$:\n\n$$\nD: \\sin x \\longrightarrow 2\\cos x\n$$\n\nIt's helpful to draw a parallel with a **linear transformation** in vector spaces, \n\n$$\nT: \\mathbf v \\longrightarrow \\mathbf w\n$$\n\nwhich takes a vector $\\mathbf v$ as input and produces another vector $\\mathbf w$ as output. The analogies between operators on function spaces and transformations on vector spaces will become clearer as we progress." :tutorial/id #uuid "c46ae630-9618-5c5f-951e-b66234fab409" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4085 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "b744a42f-230c-5f20-bb57-d8014dc80f0d" :step/math-academy-id 593341 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079548}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079548 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079614 :tutorial/content "In the last example, we defined a *second-order differential operator* as\n\n$$\nD(y)=\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{2}y}{\\textrm d x^{2}}+2\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm d x} + y.\n$$\n\nUnder this notation, it is assumed that the function $y(x)$ is passed as input to the operator. \n\nWe sometimes prefer not to specify which function is being used as input. In that case, we may write the operator itself as\n\n$$\nD=\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{2}}{\\textrm d x^{2}}+2\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x} + 1.\n$$\n\nUsing this notation, we can apply the operator to any suitable function. So, if $f(x)$ is a twice-differentiable function, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(f) & =(\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}+1)f \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}f}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+2\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}+f.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThink of this as *applying* the operator $D$ *to* the function $f.$\n\nFor the first part of this lesson, we'll restrict our attention to differential operators that are of the form\n\n$$\nD(y) = a_n\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{n}y}{\\textrm d x^{n}} + a_{n-1}\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{n-1}y}{\\textrm d x^{n-1}} + \\cdots + a_{1}\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm d x} + a_0y\n$$\n\nwhere $a_0, a_1, \\ldots, a_n$ are all constants. We'll encounter more general forms later in the lesson." :tutorial/id #uuid "d00f8f83-f07f-5df7-8695-629e5f2a3130" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14619 :tutorial/title "Notation"} :step/id #uuid "ca935020-b364-5a10-b8ff-b9f4d6c659c6" :step/math-academy-id 593342 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079549}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079549 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078588 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078335 :question/id #uuid "91e7f8e9-dc03-5203-9d33-0175960ba276" :question/math-academy-id "e-6570"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec3823db-6df8-502a-a050-b5aa169d2e9d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049332} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050271} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050789} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050902}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079163} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079223} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265819} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265863} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265866} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311886} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311887} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311888} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311889} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335781} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357496} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357498} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368209}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying Differential Operators With Constant Coefficients"} :step/id #uuid "8353cd5b-82c6-547b-8d15-2f52b7c49334" :step/math-academy-id 593343 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079550}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079550 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079613 :tutorial/content "Differential operators can be quite complex, involving products of the function $y(x)$, its derivatives, and the independent variable $x$.\n\nFor example, consider the following **third-order differential operator**:\n\n$$\nD(y)=y^2\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{3}y}{\\textrm d x^{3}} + x^2y\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm d x} + xy\n$$\n\nLet's apply this operator to the function $y(x) = x^2$. \n\n**Step 1:** Substitute $y=x^2$ into the operator expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(y) & ={y}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}+{x}^{2}y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+xy \\\\  & =({x}^{2})^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}({x}^{2})+{x}^{2}({x}^{2})\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})+x({x}^{2}) \\\\  & ={x}^{4}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}({x}^{2})+{x}^{4}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})+{x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Calculate the required derivatives step-by-step.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(y) & ={x}^{4}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(2x)+{x}^{4}(2x)+{x}^{3} \\\\  & ={x}^{4}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2)+2{x}^{5}+{x}^{3} \\\\  & ={x}^{4}\\cdot (0)+2{x}^{5}+{x}^{3} \\\\  & =2{x}^{5}+{x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we'll give the general definition of the $n$-th order differential operator and explain the difference between linear and non-linear differential operators." :tutorial/id #uuid "cd93b2d8-0c4f-53d5-b4e9-770da3bea5d4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14618 :tutorial/title "More Complex Differential Operators"} :step/id #uuid "74f88a92-ab79-5a2a-8101-1a336fba27bd" :step/math-academy-id 593346 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079554}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079551 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078589 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078336 :question/id #uuid "0fba12b1-fa78-5ff2-88e4-2a690105d321" :question/math-academy-id "e-6571"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7ff7f935-dcec-5b4c-ba1d-e0b4c9e197a4" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079164} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079165} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265818} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311890} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311891} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311892} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311893} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335780} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348247} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357497} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357499} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363038}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying Differential Operators With Functions of X as Coefficients"} :step/id #uuid "839312a2-5864-5b67-87cc-32bb715dfbb7" :step/math-academy-id 593347 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079552}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079552 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078590 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078215 :question/id #uuid "31a9b20e-d159-5ff2-94dc-54b8d7b8e620" :question/math-academy-id "e-21785"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7066d712-f0a3-554f-9d10-3d6fe4b95a18" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078867} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078868} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078869} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311894} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311895} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311901} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311902} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311904} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311919} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311920} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311926}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying Differential Operators With Multivariable Functions as Coefficients"} :step/id #uuid "ceef53f4-191f-5406-bbe5-2e4c0a833802" :step/math-academy-id 593350} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079554 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079618 :tutorial/content "The properties of a differential operator depend on its structure. A key distinction is whether an operator is **linear** or **non-linear**.\n\nA general **$n$-th order linear differential operator** has the form:\n\n$$\nL(y) = f_{n}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{n}y}{\\textrm d x^{n}} + f_{n-1}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{n-1}y}{\\textrm d x^{n-1}} + \\cdots + f_{1}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm d x} + f_0(x)y,\n$$\n\nwhere the coefficients are functions of the independent variable $x$ only.\n\nFor example, the operator\n\n$$\nD(y) = x y''' - y\n$$\n\nis a *linear operator*, since its coefficients ($x$ and $-1$) depend only on $x$.\n\nIn contrast, the operator from our previous example,\n\n$$\nD(y)=y^2 y''' + x^2y y' + xy,\n$$\n\n is *non-linear* because some of its coefficients depend on $y$. For instance, the coefficient of $y'''$ is $y^2$.\n\nOther operators are non-linear because they involve powers of derivatives or other non-linear functions of $y$. For example,\n\n$$\nD(y) = \\left(y' \\right)^2 + y\n$$\n\nis non-linear due to the $(y')^2$ term.\n\nFor the rest of this topic, we will focus primarily on linear operators, as non-linear cases are typically more complex." :tutorial/id #uuid "b85dce79-a68a-5df3-b681-6a996c10ca48" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14903 :tutorial/title "A General n-th Order Linear Differential Operator."} :step/id #uuid "ccac473f-1a73-5b81-8a15-00ee17de748f" :step/math-academy-id 608079 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079551}}] :activity/title "Differential Operators" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186078672}]}
